<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venice Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as European Commission for Democracy through Law</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> Commission, formally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> for Democracy through Law, is an advisory body of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> founded in 1990. Made up of independent experts in constitutional law, it gives opinions on constitutions and laws of member states; in 2002 it criticised the extension of the prince's powers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Commission was created in 1990, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a>, when Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> urgently needed constitutional assistance. The Italian minister Antonio Mario La Pergola conceived the idea of a group of constitutional experts and chose the name from his conviction that lasting democracies could be built only in a constitutional framework based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>. The Italian foreign minister Gianni De Michelis formally proposed it at a conference at the Giorgio Cini Foundation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 31 March and 1 April 1989.</p><p>The Committee of Ministers approved its creation as a partial agreement at a session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 19 and 20 January 1990. On 10 May 1990 ministers from 18 countries adopted its statute, and La Pergola became its first president. All member states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> soon joined, and since 2002 non-European states can also become full members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The members are senior academics, particularly in constitutional or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, supreme or constitutional court judges, or members of national parliaments. They sit in their individual capacity and are appointed for four years by the participating countries. Its secretariat is at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a>'s headquarters in Strasbourg. Its main work is to give constitutional assistance in the form of opinions on draft constitutions, amendments or other legislation.</p><p>For an opinion the Commission appoints a working group of rapporteurs, which visits the country and meets the political actors, then prepares a draft that the Commission adopts at a plenary session. The opinions are not binding but are mostly followed. The Commission describes its approach as non-directive and based on dialogue. Its code of good practice in electoral matters was adopted in October 2002.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> the Commission had an important part in developing and interpreting the constitutional law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. In 2009 it drew rare news coverage with its opinion that blasphemy should not be illegal. In Opinion 833/2015, on changes to the Polish Constitutional Tribunal, it found that a new quorum of 13 judges out of 15 went beyond established standards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was a member from 1996 until 2022, when it was expelled from both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> and the Commission after its invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Venice Commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
